      "id": "https://agronews.com.pl/artykul/brakuje-gazu-do-suszenia-kukurydzy-skupy-wstrzymuja-przyjecia-rolnicy-traca-pieniadze/",
      "url": "https://agronews.com.pl/artykul/brakuje-gazu-do-suszenia-kukurydzy-skupy-wstrzymuja-przyjecia-rolnicy-traca-pieniadze/",
      "title": "[agronews.com.pl] Propane shortages disrupt Polish corn drying during harvest season",
      "content_html": "<img src=\"https://kagiproxy.com/img/Lu66r1Kx2HePyb6agziHZgxR43bggHO7boXhE4H3Lbq3oSxk4sAoL-LsAHW40fauXcilEbdgfkalbt6WijT7U-GXYJohCPq24L_MQguhjNqchu_eQ6_02Qszt4hETy-ZhkpibxoDVjBoOQ\" style=\"width:100%;max-height:300px;object-fit:cover;\"/>\nPolish corn growers are struggling to dry harvested grain as propane supplies tighten and prices climb, prompting some collection points to stop accepting wet corn and some farmers to pause harvesting. The National Council of Agricultural Chambers asked Energy Minister Miłosz Motyka on Nov. 3 to secure fuel for the farm sector and explain the supply disruption; grain corn covers about 1.4 million hectares in Poland.\n\nIn the Lublin region, propane prices rose from about 2 zlotys per liter in January to more than 3.20 zlotys, pushing reported drying costs to 90-105 zlotys net per ton. Industry sources cited by Top Agrar pointed to Baltic storms delaying ship unloading, low inventories and contracted volumes estimated at nearly 20% below market demand. Wet corn awaiting drying risks losing quality. Farmers and regional chambers have also called for price oversight or relief; no timetable for a government response was reported.",
      "date_published": "2026-11-04T06:30:00+00:00",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "agronews.com.pl",
          "url": "https://agronews.com.pl"
