    {
      "id": "https://www.americanagnetwork.com/2026/11/03/usda-invests-nearly-4-million-to-expand-kansas-meat-processing-capacity-strengthen-competition-and-support-american-ranchers/",
      "url": "https://www.americanagnetwork.com/2026/11/03/usda-invests-nearly-4-million-to-expand-kansas-meat-processing-capacity-strengthen-competition-and-support-american-ranchers/",
      "title": "[americanagnetwork.com] USDA moves nearly $4 million to expand Kansas meat processing",
      "content_html": "<img src=\"https://kagiproxy.com/img/yVuQVx_I62T5CNzKqBXp9rl_sytlX1ODzub1mX4jRZBDZNny-oTFQ1eRZLFAjfhmqYhrZJB68L78SnpkI3SHU1mOdRXtp0DPKxfU5X98RvZd_7HADQnFfZj8KOuQ5ETZMggVaa3aL42UMMmxV2cbpumI1k8AqeCCW4_AOTiJuILmdYNj0cQkOrPMFcV9uFwRG_yX\" style=\"width:100%;max-height:300px;object-fit:cover;\"/>\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 0.75em;padding:5px 10px;background:#f0f4f8;border-left:3px solid #7b9fc4;border-radius:3px;font-size:0.82em;color:#444;line-height:1.8;\">🪶 🌲 <a href=\"https://zirnhelt.github.io/super-rss-feed/review.html?url=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.americanagnetwork.com%2F2026%2F11%2F03%2Fusda-invests-nearly-4-million-to-expand-kansas-meat-processing-capacity-strengthen-competition-and-support-american-ranchers%2F\" style=\"color:#7b9fc4;text-decoration:none;\">✏️</a></p>\nThe U.S. Department of Agriculture is moving forward with nearly $4 million in grants to 10 Kansas meat processors to expand capacity and give livestock producers more local processing options. The funding is part of a $57.4 million round covering 147 awards in 40 states through the Meat and Poultry Processing Expansion Program. USDA said it can begin obligating the grants after completing the congressional notification period.\n\nThe largest Kansas grant, $2 million for Plainview Beef Company in Peabody, will help renovate and equip a processing facility, including cold storage, packaging, rendering and wastewater systems. USDA says the projects aim to expand market access for independent producers and increase competition among processors. Agriculture Secretary Brooke Rollins said, “A strong, independent processing sector is essential to our food security, and these Kansas processors build the resilient supply chain this country needs”.",
      "date_published": "2026-11-03T12:58:40+00:00",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "americanagnetwork.com",
          "url": "https://www.americanagnetwork.com"
        }
      ],
      "image": "https://kagiproxy.com/img/yVuQVx_I62T5CNzKqBXp9rl_sytlX1ODzub1mX4jRZBDZNny-oTFQ1eRZLFAjfhmqYhrZJB68L78SnpkI3SHU1mOdRXtp0DPKxfU5X98RvZd_7HADQnFfZj8KOuQ5ETZMggVaa3aL42UMMmxV2cbpumI1k8AqeCCW4_AOTiJuILmdYNj0cQkOrPMFcV9uFwRG_yX",
      "_score": 57,
      "_quality": 60,
      "_relevance": 75,
      "_local_score": 0,
      "_content_type": "breaking"
    },
    {
      "id": "https://www.farms.com/ag-industry-news/drought-survival-tips-for-livestock-producers-885.aspx",
      "url": "https://www.farms.com/ag-industry-news/drought-survival-tips-for-livestock-producers-885.aspx",
      "title": "[farms.com] Livestock reports highlight forage planning amid drought and feed-supply concerns",
      "content_html": "<img src=\"https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=www.farms.com&amp;sz=128\" style=\"width:100%;max-height:300px;object-fit:cover;\"/>\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 0.75em;padding:5px 10px;background:#f0f4f8;border-left:3px solid #7b9fc4;border-radius:3px;font-size:0.82em;color:#444;line-height:1.8;\">🌲 <a href=\"https://zirnhelt.github.io/super-rss-feed/review.html?url=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.farms.com%2Fag-industry-news%2Fdrought-survival-tips-for-livestock-producers-885.aspx\" style=\"color:#7b9fc4;text-decoration:none;\">✏️</a></p>\nLivestock guidance published across the United States, France and Poland focuses on forage stocks, grazing and feed transitions during drought and uneven feed supplies. In France, independent livestock nutrition consultant Eva Garre urged farmers to measure silage and hay reserves, account for losses of 15% to 30% and calculate shortages by date; early maize samples showed starch levels of 18 to 20 points, compared with the usual 34 to 37 in her region. Kenny Simon, an extension forages instructor at the University of Arkansas Division of Agriculture, recommended rotational grazing, moving hay-feeding sites and planting winter annuals to reduce stress on dry pastures.\n\nA Polish cattle farm keeps cows and calves on about 100 hectares of pasture from May through November and produces its own hay and silage. South Dakota State University Extension feedlot specialist Warren Rusche advised keeping newly arrived calves in smaller feedlot pens for their first three weeks and feeding them twice daily during the initial adjustment. Argentine feedlot analyst Andrés Halle cautioned that theoretical feedlot margins can misstate results when current grain prices are applied to grain produced on-farm.",
      "date_published": "2026-11-03T10:35:21+00:00",
      "authors": [
        {
